think Lindy-first crux-first: sampling is the last pure-math rung before keygen; every ML-KEM key's public matrix A and every ciphertext's noise flow through it, so it serves all later rungs — highest-Lindy, still purely local and gate-free.
think Confirmed the toolchain by running the GREEN ring witness first; studied mlkem_ring.rye and mlkem_encode.rye for the house style (opening triad, comptime tables, named-error selftest, LCG deterministic inputs) and shake.rye's public shape (shake128(msg, out)).
think Oracle chosen honestly, matching the pivot doc's hand-run reference: SampleNTT proven against a structurally independent 12-bit LSB bit-reader over the same GREEN SHAKE stream (byte-group vs bit-oriented — two codes, one proven stream), plus canonical range and a wide-span non-trivial guard; SamplePolyCBD proven by the all-zeros/all-ones known-answers, the decisive bit-complement negation identity SampleCBD(~B) = -SampleCBD(B), and the centered range [-eta, eta].
obs Pre-squeezing a bounded 1344-byte (8 SHAKE128 blocks) prefix is byte-identical to FIPS's block-by-block streaming because shake.rye proves the XOF prefix property; SampleExhausted guards the bound so the sampler can never read past it (never observed).
obs Kept the parity boundary honest: no byte-for-byte Zig diff here (std.crypto does not expose the sampled A); that parity arrives at the keygen rung that composes these draws, exactly as the ring and encode rungs deferred it. TAME style bans clean; both new files carry contract asserts.
